sources:
    - id: adh2010
      title: 'Synthetic Control Methods for Comparative Case Studies: Estimating the Effect of California''s Tobacco Control Program'
      authors: Alberto Abadie, Alexis Diamond, Jens Hainmueller, Journal of the American Statistical Association 105(490)
      year: 2010
      finding: 'Donor pool of 38 states (4 with large tobacco programmes, 7 with tax rises of 50 cents or more, and DC left out). Synthetic California = Utah 0.334, Nevada 0.234, Montana 0.199, Colorado 0.164, Connecticut 0.069; matched on retail price, log income, share aged 15-24, beer consumption and sales in 1975, 1980, 1988. By 2000 per-capita sales were about 26 packs lower than in synthetic California; almost 20 packs a year on average over 1989-2000, about 25%. Placebo runs: post/pre-1989 MSPE ratio about 130 for California, larger than all 38 control states, p = 1/39 = 0.026; keeping only states fitted within 2x California''s pre-1989 MSPE (19 controls), California''s gap is the lowest, 1 in 20 = 5%.'
      doi: 10.1198/jasa.2009.ap08746
    - id: ow2005
      title: The Tax Burden on Tobacco (state cigarette sales per capita, 1970-2000), as distributed with the Synth package
      authors: Orzechowski and Walker
      year: 2005
      finding: 'Packs sold per person: California 123.0 (1970), 90.1 (1988), 41.6 (2000); average of the 38 donor states 120.1 (1970), 113.8 (1988). Figures for the blends, misses and placebo re-runs in this lecture are computed from these data.'

    path prop_line dashed thick #8f887c points [[1988.5,5],[1988.5,145]] data true hidden true
    text prop_lab "Prop 99" #8f887c at.data [1993.5,140] cites ["adh2010"] font_size 34 hidden true
    text r123 "123" #2f6b8f at.data [1972.2,111] cites ["ow2005"] font_size 34 hidden true
    text r90 "90" #2f6b8f at.data [1987,78] cites ["ow2005"] font_size 34 hidden true
    text r42 "42" #2f6b8f at.data [1999.5,30] cites ["ow2005"] font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "A state with no twin"

Did California's 1989 tax on cigarettes make people smoke less, and if so, by how much?
    draw axes line_1

Here is the state's own record: packs sold per person each year, about a hundred (@draw r123@) and twenty-three in 1970.
    highlight line_1

By 1988 th (@draw r90@)at was down to ninety.

That November, voters passed Proposition 99 (@draw prop_lab@): twenty-five cents more tax on every pack from Janu (@point at.data [1989,100] gesture tap@)ary 1989, with the money spent on anti-smoking campaigns and health education.
    draw prop_line

(@draw r42@) After it, sales kept falling, to about forty-two packs by 2000.
    animate stage 1 duration 3

The naive answer reads the effect straight off the line: forty-eig (@highlight r90 r42@)ht packs fewer, all of it thanks to the tax.
    arrow drop thick #8f887c from.data [1990,90] to.data [1990,44]
    text drop_lab "−48" #8f887c at.data [1986.4,60] font_size 40

But look to the left of the vote: sales had been falling for a decade before anyone cast a ballot.
    point at.data [1978,124] gesture underline duration 4

So part of those forty-eight packs would have gone anyway. The question is how many.
    erase drop drop_lab

What we need is California after 1988 without the tax: its untreated outcome, written Y of zero, year by year.
    text qmark "?" #b5482e at.data [1995,95] font_size 90
    math y0 at.data [1995,118] tex "{Y_{CA,t}(0)}" size 48 colors {"{Y_{CA,t}(0)}":"#b5482e"}

And with one treated state, that path is never observed, so something else has to stand in for it.
    highlight qmark

    erase qmark y0

The obvious stand-in is the other states: here, the average of thirty-eight that ran no larg (@point at.data [1980,138] gesture underline@)e anti-smoking programme and raised no big tax in these years.
    draw line_2

In 1970 the two sit close: a hundred and twenty-thr (@highlight r123@)ee packs for California, a hundred and twenty for the average.
    text r120 "120" #b5482e at.data [1971.6,137] cites ["ow2005"] font_size 34

But by 1988 they are twenty-four packs apart, because California's sales started falling earlier, and fell faster.
    path gap88 #8a5fa8 points [[1987.2,91],[1988,91],[1988,113],[1987.2,113]] data true style.stroke_width 4
    text gap88_lab "24 apart" #8a5fa8 at.data [1983,100] cites ["ow2005"] font_size 34

    quiz "Suppose we used the 38-state average anyway, in a difference-in-differences. The effect it gives the tax would be…" intro "A quick check."
        right: Too big: California was already falling faster than the average before 1989, and the comparison hands that head start to the tax.
        wrong: Before 1989, which line was falling faster, and where does a difference-in-differences put that extra fall afterwards?
        * Too small
        * About right
        + Too big

So a fair stand-in has to do what the average cannot: move like California before 1989.
    highlight line_1

And no single state tracks it closely either, which is why the method we need doe (@highlight line_1@)s not pick a state. It builds one.
    fade line_2 r120 gap88 gap88_lab to 0.25

