    card title "The gap after 1989"

So what does synthetic California say the tax did? Here is the real California again, from 1970 to 2000.
    draw axes line_1

The five-state blend has been fitted to everything left of the vote. To its righ (@point at.data [1994,110] gesture circle duration 3@)t, it plays the California that never passed the tax.
    draw prop_line prop_lab

    ask "Synthetic California matches the real one up to 1988. Draw where you think it goes by 2000." intro "Your guess first." on line_2 from 1988 store g default "60"
        right: Close: you ended near {g.end:0} packs, and the blend ends at about {g.true:0}.
        wrong: You ended near {g.end:0}; the blend ends at about {g.true:0} packs, still sliding, but slowly.

The shaded band was the distance between your line and the blend. Without the tax, the blend says, sales would have kept sliding slowly, to about  (@draw r67@)sixty-seven packs per person in 2000.
    highlight line_2

The real California fell much f (@draw r42@)aster, to forty-two.
    highlight line_1

The gap in 2000, about twenty-six packs per person a year, is the estimated effect of Proposition 99.
    draw gap_mark gap_lab

In symbols, the effect in each year is what California sold, minus what its synthetic twin sold.
    draw tau

And averaged over all twelve years from 1989 to 2000, the gap is almost t (@point at.data [1994,70] gesture underline duration 3@)wenty packs a year: about a quarter of what synthetic California bought.
    draw avg

No standard error comes with that number. How sure we can be is a question of its own, and it needs its own trick.
    highlight gap_lab

It also measures a package, not a tax alone: the price ris (@highlight prop_lab@)e, the anti-smoking campaigns the money paid for, and some hundred and forty local laws  (@highlight gap_lab@)that followed it, making restaurants smoke-free.
    erase tau avg

And it means that only if three things hold. First, nothing else hit California alo (@highlight line_1@)ne after 1988, or that shock would land in the gap too.
    draw need_box need_line_1

Second, the donor states were left alone, which is why states wi (@highlight line_2@)th big programmes of their own, or tax rises of fifty cents or more, were kept out of the pool.
    draw need_line_2

Third, the weights that match (@point at.data [1980,122] gesture underline duration 2@)ed nineteen years before the tax would (@point at.data [1995,80] gesture underline duration 2@) have kept matching after it.
    draw need_line_3

One more caveat: sales are counted from tax paid, so smuggling would look like less smoking. The authors argue California is too far from cheap states for that to matter much.
    highlight r42

Of all this, the main thing we can check against data is the fit before 1989.
    point at.data [1979,124] gesture underline duration 4

    quiz "Suppose a synthetic Texas, built to study a 1995 law, misses Texas's own sales before 1995 by twelve packs a year. What should we make of its gap after 1995?" intro "A new case."
        right: Little: a blend that cannot reproduce the past it was fitted to may simply go on missing after 1995, and the gap would show that miss, not the law.
        wrong: Which part of a synthetic control can be checked most directly, and what does a twelve-pack miss say about it?
        * It is the law's effect
        + Little: the stand-in failed its main test
        * Half of it is the law's effect

So the fit before the policy is not decoration. It is the main evidence that the stand-in is fair.
    highlight line_1 line_2

