    card title "Is 26 packs unusual?"

A gap of twenty-six packs looks large. But could the method throw up a gap that size by chance?
    draw axes ty30n ty0 ty30 tx1970 tx1988 tx2000 zero prop_line

Here is the gap year by year: close to zero before the vote, then sinking towards minus twenty-six.
    draw ca_gap ca_lab

In a trial, a standard error answers that. Oregon's 2008 Medicaid lottery, for one, drew thousands of names at random, so the play of chance could be measured.
    highlight zero

But California is one state, picked by its own voters, so there is no draw to repeat and no standard error.
    highlight ca_gap

So ask a different question: if we pretended another state had passed Proposition 99 in 1988, how large a gap would the method find there?
    point at.data [1994,20] gesture circle duration 4

Run the whole recipe on each donor state in turn, as if it had been treated. These grey li (@highlight rerun@)nes are our own re-run on the same data, matching on sales alone; the n (@highlight ca_gap@)umbers on the right will be the paper's.
    draw plac_good plac_bad rerun parallel true

Four states lie outside the band the other states span, New Hampshire, the heaviest smoker, above all, so no blend can match them. They are left out of this picture, though the ratio test later keeps all thirty-eight.
    highlight rerun

Others are matched poorly too. Their lines already wander before 1989, so a wide gap afterwards says little about them.
    highlight plac_bad

So keep only the states whose past was matched almost as well as California's: at most twice its misfit.
    erase plac_bad

In our re-run, twenty-one states survive that cut, and among them California's gap is the lowest from the mid-1990s on.
    highlight ca_gap

In the paper's version, nineteen states survive, plus California makes twenty, so a gap as extreme as California's would turn up by chance one time in twenty.
    text res_h "Abadie et al. 2010" #8f887c cites ["adh2010"] x 845 y 590 font_size 28
    text res1a "Fit within 2×:" x 845 y 520 font_size 32
    text res1b "lowest of 20" #8a5fa8 cites ["adh2010"] x 845 y 478 font_size 32
    math res1c cites ["adh2010"] tex "p = 1/20 = 0.05" size 32 x 845 y 432

To avoid choosing a cut-off  (@point at.data [1995,-20] gesture circle duration 2@)at all, the authors also div (@point at.data [1980,0] gesture underline duration 2@)ide each state's misfit after 1988 by its misfit before.
    text res2a "Misfit ratio:" x 845 y 350 font_size 32

California's ratio is about a hundred and thirty, larger than any of the thirty-eight others: one chance in thirty-nine, a p-value of about 0.026.
    text res2b "1st of 39" #8a5fa8 cites ["adh2010"] x 845 y 308 font_size 32
    math res2c cites ["adh2010"] tex "p = 1/39 \\approx 0.026" size 32 x 845 y 262

That number treats California as if chance had picked it from the thirty-nine. It says how unusual the gap is, not how the gap came about.
    highlight res2c effect underline

    quiz "A city's soda tax is studied with synthetic control and nine donor cities. Its gap is the most extreme of all ten. What is the smallest p-value placebo runs can give it?" intro "One more case."
        right: One in ten: with ten cities, even the most extreme rank turns up by chance once in ten, so a small donor pool can never give strong evidence.
        wrong: Count every city the label could have landed on, the treated one included.
        * 0.01
        * 0.026
        + 0.1

With N units in the comparison, the smallest p-value placebos can give is one over N. That is why nineteen survivors plus California can do no better than one in twenty, and thirty-eight donors plus California no better than one in thirty-nine.
    highlight res1c res2c effect underline

So how much did Proposition 99 cut smoking? About twenty-six packs per person a year b (@highlight ca_gap@)y 2000, against a California built from five other states.
    erase plac_good prop_line rerun res_h res1a res1b res1c res2a res2b res2c

The blend earned its place before the policy, by (@point at.data [1979,0] gesture underline duration 3@) matching California's nineteen years before anyone voted.
    draw takeaway_1

Its weights are never negative and add to one, so the stand-in (@highlight ca_gap@) never strays beyond what real states did.
    draw takeaway_2

And with one treated state, how sure we are comes from (@point at.data [1997,-24] gesture circle duration 3@) ranking it among placebos, not from a standard error.
    draw takeaway_3

California had no twin, so economists assembled one, and the tax showed up in the space between them.
    highlight ca_gap

## Where next
end_page: true

